OpenBOR Android Standalone Game Guide
=====================================

Packaging model
---------------

OpenBOR games ship as standalone Android applications. One APK contains one
OpenBOR engine build and one game PAK. Players launch the game by its title and
icon; no PAK picker or shared OpenBOR library is involved.

Every game needs its own Android application ID. Distinct IDs let several
OpenBOR games remain installed together, with separate PAKs, saves, logs, and
screenshots.

The source tree without a game configuration is an engine build template, not
an end-user game package.


Requirements
------------

- JDK 17
- Android SDK platform 35
- Android Build Tools 36.0.0
- Android NDK 21.4.7075529
- 64-bit ARM Android device or emulator for testing

The Gradle wrapper is included. Existing build.bat and build.sh scripts can set
up or invoke the development build, while the commands below make each build
type explicit.


1. Configure the game
---------------------

Copy game.properties.example to game.properties in engine/android, then edit
the values:

    game.applicationId=io.github.creator.game
    game.name=Game Title
    game.versionCode=1
    game.versionName=1.0.0
    game.pak=path/to/game.pak
    game.pakName=game.pak

Property details:

game.applicationId
    Permanent Android identity for this game. Use two or more lowercase
    segments, normally a reversed domain you control. Creators without a domain
    can use a stable form such as io.github.account.gametitle. Never reuse one
    ID for unrelated games, and never change the ID after publishing a game.

game.name
    Title displayed by Android.

game.versionCode
    Positive whole number used by Android to order releases. Increase it for
    every update. The launcher also uses this value as the installed-data
    revision, without changing the PAK file name.

game.versionName
    Player-facing release label, such as 1.0.0.

game.pak
    Source PAK path. Relative paths start in engine/android; absolute paths also
    work. Use forward slashes in Windows paths, for example C:/Games/MyGame.pak.

game.pakName
    Stable file name created in the application's Paks directory. Keep it
    unchanged across updates because OpenBOR derives save and configuration
    names from the PAK file name. Use the name expected by any game scripts that
    inspect their archive, for example SORX.pak or 1.0.0.pak.

Leaving game.pak empty uses app/src/main/assets/bor.pak for compatibility with
the original packaging workflow. Gradle stages either source as assets/bor.pak,
stores it uncompressed in the package, and rejects a missing or empty PAK for a
release build.

The engine Java namespace remains org.openbor.engine. Creators change only
game.applicationId; changing the namespace would break the source package and
does not provide additional install isolation.


2. Add game branding
--------------------

Replace the launcher artwork under app/src/main/res/mipmap-* while preserving
the existing resource names. Replace app/src/main/res/drawable/background.png
if a custom loading background is desired. game.name supplies the application
label.


3. Build and test a debug APK
-----------------------------

From engine/android:

Windows:

    gradlew.bat clean assembleDebug

Linux or macOS:

    ./gradlew clean assembleDebug

The output is:

    app/build/outputs/apk/debug/OpenBOR.apk

Install and test the complete game, including first launch, second launch,
save creation, controller input, and an update over the installed test build.


4. Create a release key
-----------------------

Every installable APK must be signed. One game must keep the same release key
for its entire update lifetime. Store the keystore and passwords securely, with
an offline backup. Losing a self-managed signing key can permanently prevent
future updates.

Use make-my-key.bat on Windows or make-my-key.sh on Linux/macOS, or create a key
with Android Studio. Then create engine/android/keystore.properties:

    storePassword=your_store_password
    keyPassword=your_key_password
    keyAlias=your_alias
    storeFile=../my-key.jks

storeFile is resolved from engine/android/app. The example points to
engine/android/my-key.jks.

Never commit keystore.properties, the keystore, or passwords. Existing ignore
rules exclude keystore.properties and *.jks. Release validation also rejects
debug.keystore and the standard androiddebugkey alias.


5. Build the release
--------------------

From engine/android:

Windows:

    gradlew.bat clean assembleRelease

Linux or macOS:

    ./gradlew clean assembleRelease

The signed APK is written under:

    app/build/outputs/apk/release/

Its file name uses game.name and game.versionName. Google Play distribution can
use bundleRelease instead of assembleRelease, followed by Play App Signing and
the normal Play Console release process.

Release validation stops the build when it finds:

- the default org.openbor.engine application ID
- an invalid application ID
- the default OpenBOR title
- a missing or empty PAK
- an invalid version code or version name
- missing signing settings or keystore
- the Android debug certificate


Updates and parallel installs
-----------------------------

For an update to an existing game:

1. Keep game.applicationId unchanged.
2. Keep the same release signing key.
3. Keep game.pakName unchanged.
4. Increase game.versionCode.
5. Set the new game.versionName.
6. Point game.pak to the updated PAK and rebuild.

Android treats a changed application ID as a different app. Matching IDs with
different signing keys cannot update one another. Matching identity, signing
key, and an increased version code produce a normal in-place update.

For another OpenBOR game, create another configuration with another application
ID and signing policy. Both games can then remain installed because Android
assigns each ID its own app-specific storage.


Runtime storage and security
----------------------------

The launcher copies the bundled PAK on a background thread into the Paks folder
under Android app-specific external storage. Copying uses a 64 KiB buffer,
flushes and synchronizes the completed file, validates its size, then publishes
it with a same-directory rename. The engine starts only after the stream closes.

Only LauncherActivity is exported to other applications. GameActivity is
private and starts only after the launcher prepares game data. No broad storage
permission is requested.

Android backup rules preserve normal user data such as Saves while excluding
regenerable or oversized Paks, Logs, and ScreenShots directories. Uninstalling
an app can still remove its app-specific files, so never treat device storage or
Android backup as the creator's only copy of important data.


Troubleshooting
---------------

"This APK does not contain game data"
    game.pak points to the wrong file, or app/src/main/assets/bor.pak is absent.
    Correct the source and rebuild.

Release validation fails on application ID
    Choose a unique lowercase ID with two or more segments. Do not change an ID
    already used for a published build.

Android refuses to install an update
    Confirm the application ID and signing key match the installed version, then
    increase game.versionCode.

The old game data still launches during local testing
    Increase game.versionCode and rebuild. The version code is the package data
    revision boundary; game.pakName remains stable.

The first launch takes time
    First launch must copy the bundled PAK into the engine's filesystem storage.
    Later launches reuse the validated installed copy for that version code.


